{"name":"Antimony","hex":"#c7c8c1","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"antimony","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A chemical element (symbol Sb, from Latin stibium) with an atomic number of 51: in its stable allotrope, a lustrous gray and very brittle metal.","The alloy stibnite."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/antimony"}],"entries":[{"title":"Antimony","blocks":[{"text":"Antimony is a chemical element with the symbol Sb and atomic number 51. A lustrous grey metal or metalloid, it occurs in nature mainly in the form of the sulfide mineral stibnite. Antimony compounds have been known since ancient history and were powdered for use as cosmetics and as medicine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Antimony-4.jpg/330px-Antimony-4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antimony"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Antimon.PNG/500px-Antimon.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Antimon.PNG/500px-Antimon.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A vial containing the metallic allotrope of antimony","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntimon.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Antimony_massive.jpg/330px-Antimony_massive.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Antimony_massive.jpg/330px-Antimony_massive.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Native antimony with oxidation products","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntimony_massive.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/SbAs_lattice.png/500px-SbAs_latti